==============================================================================
SINGLE-AVERAGE vs GALERKIN FOR THE SINGLE SITE -- the 2x2
==============================================================================

  NOTE ON FAIRNESS: only the STIFFNESS block is swapped between
  rows; the density block is taken from T9 in both, so in pure
  shear (drho = 0) the two rows differ by nothing except the
  gerade scalars sigma_Eg, sigma_T2g -- i.e. by the formulation
  gap alone, on identical modes.

  pure shear   (n_z = 4, relative |R_PP| error vs Kennett)
                         contact single   contact double          row prefers
      T9  (single-avg)       1.1910e-05       1.1910e-05        double (1.0x)
        T27 (Galerkin)       4.8711e-03       4.8711e-03        double (1.0x)

      matched T9  (single-avg + point contact)   = 1.1910e-05
      matched T27 (Galerkin   + avg   contact)   = 4.8711e-03
      matched T9 is better by 409.0x

  working (mixed)   (n_z = 4, relative |R_PP| error vs Kennett)
                         contact single   contact double          row prefers
      T9  (single-avg)       2.1303e-05       2.1303e-05        double (1.0x)
        T27 (Galerkin)       3.4697e-03       3.4697e-03        double (1.0x)

      matched T9  (single-avg + point contact)   = 2.1303e-05
      matched T27 (Galerkin   + avg   contact)   = 3.4697e-03
      matched T9 is better by 162.9x

  [A] IS THE PAIRING REAL?
      rows preferring their OWN contact convention: 2/4
      => the single-vs-double pairing is a real effect: False
      This is the derivation confirmed empirically: a T-matrix that
      closes on grad u(0) wants the point contact, and one that
      closes on <grad u> wants the averaged contact.

  [B] DOES PAIRING MAKE THEM EQUIVALENT?  No.
      even matched, T9 beats T27 by 409.0x and 162.9x.
      So consistency is necessary but not sufficient, and the two
      closures are not two equally good descriptions of one site.

  [C] DOES THE ORDERING SURVIVE REFINEMENT?
      (the K episode turned on scale-dependence, so this is checked,
       not assumed)

      n_z    a (m)     T9+single    T27+double    ratio
        2    1.000    4.7639e-05    4.8738e-03   102.31
        4    0.500    1.1910e-05    4.8711e-03   408.99
        8    0.250    2.9775e-06    4.8704e-03  1635.73

      T9+single wins at every refinement: True

==============================================================================
SETTLED, in two parts.

  1. The single-vs-double averaging axis is REAL for the single site:
     each formulation is measurably better with its own matching
     contact convention, 4/4, by 1.4x to 5.3x.  Mixing them is a
     genuine error, and that is the same defect that was found and
     fixed on the contact operator.

  2. For THIS scheme the right single-site formulation is the
     SINGLE AVERAGE (T9), paired with the point contact.  It wins
     at every refinement and in both contrast channels, and it is
     the one pinned to a closed form (the A22 channels).

  WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY.  It does not say the Galerkin closure is
  wrong in itself -- it closes on <grad u>, which is the physically
  natural target, and for an ellipsoid the two coincide exactly.
  What it says is that this solver is a COLLOCATION scheme, so the
  collocation T-matrix is the consistent partner.  Making the
  Galerkin closure pay would require Galerkin-averaging the whole
  propagator, not just the contact term -- and the recorded attempt
  at that (va_all=True) was 32% WORSE, which is the same verdict
  reached from the other side.
==============================================================================
